BT /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 56 760 Td (property managers who would pay to offer the service to their tenants. The shop owner pays) Tj T* (nothing up front for the locker, as we cover the hardware cost and own the unit.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Our current pricing is around a dollar a pickup with the shop owner keeping roughly forty) Tj T* (percent, though this is still being tested. We expect the hardware to pay for itself over time) Tj T* (as volume grows, but we dont have enough data yet to say how long that will take.) Tj T* () Tj T* (COMPETITION) Tj T* () Tj T* (The space is crowded. There are large national locker companies, carrier owned lockers, retail) Tj T* (partnership pickup points, and apartment lobby locker systems. There are also package concierge) Tj T* (apps and neighborhood pickup point networks that use shops to hold packages by hand. We think) Tj T* (our difference is the focus on underserved neighborhoods, the use of smart lock hardware so) Tj T* (shop owners do not need to watch packages, and the fee model that gives shops a reason to) Tj T* (participate. We know bigger players could copy us, but we think our local relationships and) Tj T* (focus will be a moat.) Tj T* () Tj T* (THE TEAM) Tj T* () Tj T* (Devontae Ricks is the CEO and handles business development, shop relationships, and) Tj T* (fundraising. He studies business at our university and previously worked part time at a corner) Tj T* (store owned by his uncle, so he understands the shop owner side.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Marisol Quenneville is the CTO and leads the app and the electronics. She is a computer) Tj T* (engineering major and built our first working prototype of the locker door and lock controller.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Tobias Wangari-Holt is in charge of operations and design. He studies industrial design and is) Tj T* (responsible for the physical unit, installation, and the customer experience.) Tj T* () Tj T* (We also have informal advisors from our university, though we have not signed any formal) Tj T* (agreements with them.) Tj T* () Tj T* (MILESTONES AND FINANCIALS) Tj T* () Tj T* (We have registered the company, although the legal entity is not formed yet and we are working) Tj T* (on that step. We have a website. We built a working prototype and installed a first unit in one) Tj T* (laundromat near campus, and our first client has paid us. We have a small number of residents) Tj T* (signed up and a handful of packages have gone through the locker so far. Early feedback has) Tj T* (been positive, though the sample is small.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Our costs so far are mostly the hardware parts, the cellular service, and some small expenses) Tj T* (paid by the founders out of pocket. Revenue to date is very small, just the first payment and a) Tj T* (few pickup fees. We project that with additional lockers we could reach breakeven in a) Tj T* (reasonable time, and we expect revenue to grow quickly once we sign more shops, but we have not) Tj T* (finished a detailed financial model.) Tj T* () Tj T* (WHAT WE ARE ASKING FOR) Tj T* () Tj T* (We are applying to this competition for prize funding to build more locker units and install) Tj T* (them in additional shops, which we think will let us prove the model with real numbers. We) Tj T* (would use funds for hardware manufacturing, cellular and software costs, legal setup of the) Tj T* (company, and some marketing in the target neighborhoods. We would also value mentorship from) Tj T* (judges and experienced entrepreneurs about hardware, pricing, and how to approach carriers and) Tj T* (property managers. Finally, we are looking for introductions to local business associations) Tj T* (that could help us reach more shop owners.) Tj T* () Tj T* (CONCLUSION) Tj T* () Tj T* (Pawnshop Lockers has the potential to make package delivery safer and more convenient in) Tj T* (neighborhoods that big companies overlook, while giving local shops a new source of income. We) Tj ET
endstream
